WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.311
Political donation system.

00:00:02.261 --> 00:00:04.159
The money pipeline.

00:00:04.159 --> 00:00:08.306
Canada banned corporate and union donations in 2006.

00:00:08.306 --> 00:00:14.352
Individual contributions are capped at one thousand seven hundred and twenty-five dollars per year per party.

00:00:14.352 --> 00:00:16.875
On the surface, this looks fair.

00:00:16.875 --> 00:00:21.079
In practice, four mechanisms ensure institutional money dominates.

00:00:21.079 --> 00:00:28.738
First: riding associations can accumulate funds over years between elections, creating war chests that favour established parties.

00:00:28.738 --> 00:00:38.420
Second: third-party spending groups can spend millions on advertising during elections as long as they don't coordinate with parties — a distinction that is nearly impossible to enforce.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:45.590
Third: leadership races have separate contribution limits, allowing candidates to receive additional funds from the same donors.

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:56.659
Fourth: the tax credit system provides a seventy-five percent credit on the first four hundred dollars donated — effectively tripling the value of small donations, but only for those with taxable income to offset.

00:00:56.659 --> 00:00:59.102
The system appears democratic.

00:00:59.102 --> 00:01:02.784
The mechanisms ensure it serves institutional interests.

00:01:02.784 --> 00:01:08.306
Four mechanisms that ensure institutional money dominates despite individual contribution limits.

00:01:08.306 --> 00:01:11.897
The political financing layer of institutional capture.

00:01:11.897 --> 00:01:18.011
The donation system is the mechanism that ensures captured institutions reproduce captured political leadership.

00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:21.909
Riding associations maintain established party dominance.

00:01:21.909 --> 00:01:25.579
Third-party spending amplifies institutional interests.

00:01:25.579 --> 00:01:29.511
Leadership races select candidates from specific networks.

00:01:29.511 --> 00:01:32.215
Tax credits favour wealthy donors.

00:01:32.215 --> 00:01:46.965
Together, these mechanisms ensure that the political system produces leaders from the same institutional ecosystem documented across this site — global finance, corporate boards, lobbying networks — while structurally disadvantaging candidates who represent communities outside that ecosystem.

00:01:46.965 --> 00:01:51.272
The system appears democratic because individuals can donate equally.

00:01:51.272 --> 00:01:55.454
The mechanisms ensure that institutional money dominates outcomes.
